## II Amplitudes in the first region

This section presents the next main result of this paper: a simple formula for the \(n\)-point single-minus amplitudes (21) with partially restricted kinematics within the half-collinear regime.

### Restricted kinematics within the half-collinear regime

We define the kinematic region \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) by the condition that, with \(\bar{\lambda}_{\dot{\alpha}}=\omega(1,\tilde{z})\), there exists at least one \(\mathsf{SO}(2,2)\) frame in which

\[\omega_{1}<0,\qquad\omega_{a}>0,\qquad a\in\{2,\ldots,n\}. \tag{33}\]

This region is fully consistent with the half-collinear regime where all \(\langle ij\rangle=0\) and further restricts the kinematics within that regime. Unlike in Minkowski signature, we note that in Klein signature, there is no invariant meaning to a particle having positive frequency. Nonetheless, \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) is \(\mathsf{SO}(2,2)\)-invariant because we only ask that there exists _some_ frame in which (33) holds. Geometrically, it amounts to requiring that there exists some straight line through the origin in \(\mathbb{R}^{2}\) such that \(\bar{\lambda}_{1}\) lies on one side and all other \(\bar{\lambda}_{a}\) lie on the other. \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) describes a single ingoing self-dual gluon decaying to \(n-1\) outgoing anti-self-dual gluons, where ingoing/outgoing refers to the frame in which the inequalities (33) hold.

Interestingly, we will see that the amplitudes dramatically simplify in \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\), where certain sign functions become independent of the frequencies \(\omega_{k}\). In particular,

\[\mathsf{sg}_{ij}=\mathsf{sg}\,\tilde{z}_{ij},\qquad\mathsf{sg}_{1j}=\mathsf{ sg}\,\tilde{z}_{j1}\qquad\forall\,i,j\geq 2. \tag{34}\]

Note however that the \(\omega_{k}\) cannot be eliminated from expressions such as \(\mathsf{sg}_{2,34}\).

### Concrete examples

In region \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\), using (34), momentum conservation and spinor identities, one may show that the long expressions of the previous section dramatically simplify to

\[A_{123}|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}} =\frac{1}{2}(\mathsf{sg}_{12}+\mathsf{sg}_{23}), \tag{35}\] \[A_{1234}|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}} =\frac{1}{4}(\mathsf{sg}_{12}+\mathsf{sg}_{23})(\mathsf{sg}_{3 4}+\mathsf{sg}_{41}),\] (36) \[A_{12345}|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}} =\frac{1}{8}(\mathsf{sg}_{12}+\mathsf{sg}_{23})\big{(}\mathsf{ sg}_{34}+\mathsf{sg}_{1,23}\big{)}\] (37) \[\qquad\qquad(\mathsf{sg}_{45}+\mathsf{sg}_{51}),\] \[A_{123456}|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}} =\frac{1}{16}(\mathsf{sg}_{12}+\mathsf{sg}_{23})\big{(}\mathsf{ sg}_{34}+\mathsf{sg}_{1,23}\big{)}\] (38) \[\big{(}\mathsf{sg}_{45}+\mathsf{sg}_{1,234}\big{)}(\mathsf{sg}_ {56}+\mathsf{sg}_{61}).\]

This suggests the possibility that there may exist a shorter formula for all \(n\).

### General formula

A conjecture that extends the pattern (35)-(38) to all \(n\)-particle amplitudes in region \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) is

\[A_{1\cdots n}\big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=\frac{1}{2^{n-2}}\prod_{m=2}^{n-1}\big{(} \mathsf{sg}_{m,m+1}+\mathsf{sg}_{1,2\cdots m}\big{)}. \tag{39}\]

How sensible is this guess? Regarding requirements (24)-(28), the region \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) is clearly not cyclically invariant, as it singles out particle 1. However, we can trivially construct a cyclically invariant answer by using cyclicity to extend (39) to other regions \(\mathcal{R}_{k}\) where only particle \(k\) has \(\omega_{k}<0\). The remaining four conditions, when combined with cyclicity, do impose quite nontrivial constraints within \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\). These constraints are all obeyed by our solution (39). In fact, in this form, it is direct to check the soft theorem (28) in the last label, and with some more work, in any other label but 1.

The simplified form (21) has a simple interpretation: in \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\), each factor is \(\frac{1}{2}(\pm 1\pm 1)\in\{-1,0,1\}\), so \(A_{1\cdots n}|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}\) is piecewise-constant and jumps across codimension-one walls where the relevant brackets change sign. The product form simply makes those walls explicit.

The rest of this work is devoted to proving that the conjecture (39) is correct. The proof uses ideas from time-ordered perturbation theory and proceeds in three parts. First, one shows that in \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\),

\[V_{\bar{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\bar{\lambda}_{n}}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=0, \tag{40}\]

whilst \(\bar{V}_{\bar{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\bar{\lambda}_{n}}\) remains nonzero. Second, one must show that with (40), the recursion (21) collapses to become

\[A_{1\cdots n}\big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=\bar{V}_{\bar{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\bar{ \lambda}_{n}}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}. \tag{41}\]

Finally, one must show that in \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\), \(\bar{V}_{\bar{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\bar{\lambda}_{n}}\) reduces to our final formula (39). We briefly sketch each step below.

Vanishing of \(V\)

We wish to show that within \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\),

\[V_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}=0 \tag{42}\]

for \(n\geq 3\). This can be interpreted as a causality condition10 in \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\) in the frame in which (33) holds. Importantly, all the \(\omega\)'s appearing in this expression are positive. This will force at least one of the arguments of the \(\Theta\)-functions to be negative, implying (42). For each cut \(j\), write left- and right-partial sums as

Footnote 10: It is very reminiscent of the largest-time equation in time-ordered perturbation theory [23].

\[\tilde{\lambda}_{L}=\sum_{a=2}^{j}\tilde{\lambda}_{a}=\Omega_{L}( 1,\tilde{z}_{L}), \tag{43}\] \[\tilde{\lambda}_{R}=\sum_{a=j+1}^{n}\tilde{\lambda}_{a}=\Omega_{ R}(1,\tilde{z}_{R}), \tag{44}\]

with \(\Omega_{L,R}>0\) and \(\tilde{z}_{L,R}\) weighted averages of the \(\tilde{z}\)'s on each side. Then

\[\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{R}\tilde{\lambda}_{L}\right] =\Omega_{L}\Omega_{R}\tilde{z}_{R,L}, \tag{45}\] \[\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{j+1}\tilde{\lambda}_{j}\right] =\omega_{j}\omega_{j+1}\tilde{z}_{j+1,j}. \tag{46}\]

Since \(\Omega_{L}\Omega_{R}>0\) and \(\omega_{j}\omega_{j+1}>0\), the sign of the ratio in the \(\Theta\)-factor in (20) is the sign of \(\tilde{z}_{R,L}/\tilde{z}_{j+1,j}\). A weighted-variance identity implies that as \(j\) runs from 2 to \(n\), there must be at least one cut \(j^{\star}\) for which \(\tilde{z}_{R,L}\) has the _same sign_ as the adjacent increment \(\tilde{z}_{j+1,j}\). For that \(j^{\star}\), the ratio is positive, so the corresponding factor \(\Theta(-\text{positive})=0\), and thus the whole product in (20) vanishes.

#### iii.2.2 Collapsing the recursion

We have in fact shown something slightly more general: for _every_ consecutive \(S\subset\{2,\dots,n\}\) with \(|S|\geq 2\),

\[V_{\tilde{\lambda}_{S_{1}}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{S_{k}}}=0. \tag{47}\]

Then, from (19), we find that on \(\mathcal{R}_{1}\),

\[\left.\bar{A}_{S}\right|_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=0, \tag{48}\]

while singletons remain, \(\bar{A}_{i}=1\). This collapses the recursion. Using the cyclicity of the color-ordered amplitude, we may write

\[A_{1\cdots n}=A_{2\cdots n1}. \tag{49}\]

Now apply the recursion (21) to \(A_{2\cdots n1}\), _i.e._, partition the ordered set \((2,3,\dots,n)\). By (48), the only nonzero contribution is the all-singleton partition \((2|3|\cdots|n)\), so

\[A_{2\cdots n1}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=-\widehat{\mathrm{PT}}_{\tilde{ \lambda}_{2}\tilde{\lambda}_{3}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R} _{1}}. \tag{50}\]

But since \(V_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}=0\), we have \(\widehat{\mathrm{PT}}=V-\bar{V}=-\bar{V}\) for this list. Therefore,

\[A_{1\cdots n}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1}}=A_{2\cdots n1}\Big{|}_{\mathcal{R}_{1} }=\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}. \tag{51}\]

This proves the collapse of the recursion (21) to (41).

#### iii.2.3 Evaluating \(\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}\)

Last but not least, we reorganize the vertex in terms of sign functions. Recall that \(\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}\) is defined as

\[\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}=\prod_{n=2}^{n}\mathrm{ sg}_{m,m+1}\Theta\Bigg{(}\frac{\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{2\cdots m}\tilde{ \lambda}_{m+1\cdots n}\right]}{\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{m}\tilde{\lambda}_{m+1} \right]}\Bigg{)}. \tag{52}\]

By momentum conservation, \(\tilde{\lambda}_{m+1\cdots n}=-\tilde{\lambda}_{1}-\tilde{\lambda}_{2\cdots m}\), and using the antisymmetry of the bracket, (52) becomes

\[\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}=\prod_{m=2}^{n-1} \mathrm{sg}_{m,m+1}\Theta\Bigg{(}\frac{\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{1}\tilde{ \lambda}_{2\cdots m}\right]}{\left[\tilde{\lambda}_{m}\tilde{\lambda}_{m+1} \right]}\Bigg{)}. \tag{53}\]

Using the relation between the sg and \(\Theta\) functions, one readily finds that

\[\bar{V}_{\tilde{\lambda}_{2}\cdots\tilde{\lambda}_{n}}=\frac{1}{2^{n-2}}\prod _{m=2}^{n-1}\Bigl{(}\mathrm{sg}_{m,m+1}+\mathrm{sg}\Bigl{(}\left[\tilde{ \lambda}_{1}\tilde{\lambda}_{2\cdots m}\right]\Bigr{)}\Bigr{)}. \tag{54}\]

Combining (51) with (54) recovers exactly our final result (39).

